1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,559
Among our graders, no made-up number is
the one we've leaned on most, and the

2
00:00:04,559 --> 00:00:06,125
oracle couldn't vouch for it.

3
00:00:06,625 --> 00:00:11,125
An untouched changelog has no made-up
number in it, so the grader passes it, and

4
00:00:11,125 --> 00:00:12,250
that proves nothing.

5
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:17,050
What would prove something is a changelog
where a model did make up a number.

6
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:21,575
Trials like that are already saved, from a
run we paid for earlier.

7
00:00:22,075 --> 00:00:26,537
Here, you'll run your graders over trials
you've already saved, and catch a grader

8
00:00:26,537 --> 00:00:27,625
that can never fail.

9
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:32,058
Let's start with why saved trials are
worth keeping.

10
00:00:32,558 --> 00:00:36,847
Every trial you've read is an example with
a known answer: you know whether it's

11
00:00:36,847 --> 00:00:38,133
right or wrong, and why.

12
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:43,430
That makes saved trials the best material
we have for testing graders, in two ways

13
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:44,833
the oracle can't manage.

14
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:49,844
Any trial you know is bad tests a grader
that checks for absence, because the

15
00:00:49,844 --> 00:00:51,133
grader has to fail it.

16
00:00:51,633 --> 00:00:55,871
Any trial you know is good, but worded
differently from the golden patch, tests

17
00:00:55,871 --> 00:00:57,533
whether a grader is too strict.

18
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:02,476
The oracle tried one correct answer for
each stimulus, while our saved runs hold

19
00:01:02,476 --> 00:01:04,808
several, each worded a little differently.

20
00:01:05,308 --> 00:01:09,658
When a grader fails one of them, it's
objecting to wording, not to a fault.

21
00:01:10,867 --> 00:01:15,376
The example here is no made-up number,
which passes when its pattern is absent

22
00:01:15,376 --> 00:01:17,342
from the patch: a must-not grader.

23
00:01:17,842 --> 00:01:22,322
If it doesn't fail a trial where the model
wrote a number nobody gave it, the grader

24
00:01:22,322 --> 00:01:24,242
is broken, whatever the oracle said.

25
00:01:24,742 --> 00:01:29,222
That gives us a simple rule: every
must-not grader needs at least one saved

26
00:01:29,222 --> 00:01:30,417
trial that it fails.

27
00:01:30,917 --> 00:01:35,137
And when a grader and our own reading
disagree, we don't assume either is right:

28
00:01:35,137 --> 00:01:37,617
we open the evidence and the patch, and
decide.

29
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:41,733
That's the idea; here's the command that
does it.

30
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:46,258
Vally grade runs graders over trials that
are already saved.

31
00:01:46,758 --> 00:01:49,533
No agent starts, so it costs nothing.

32
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:52,508
Dash e names the eval file.

33
00:01:53,008 --> 00:01:57,672
Its graders are applied as they are in the
file now, matched to each saved trial by

34
00:01:57,672 --> 00:01:58,458
stimulus name.

35
00:01:58,958 --> 00:02:02,983
So when we change a grader, the next
re-grade uses the changed one.

36
00:02:03,483 --> 00:02:08,933
The saved trials are the run's results dot
jsonl, given to the command as its input.

37
00:02:09,433 --> 00:02:13,975
And dash dash run dir names the run's
folder, because one more piece is kept

38
00:02:13,975 --> 00:02:14,333
there.

39
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:19,008
Each trial's folder has a workspace patch
for us to read.

40
00:02:19,508 --> 00:02:23,690
If the eval file has a diff grader,
meaning one of the three graders that work

41
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:28,087
from the patch, the run also keeps a copy
of every trial's diff, in a folder named

42
00:02:28,087 --> 00:02:28,408
diffs.

43
00:02:28,908 --> 00:02:33,683
Those copies are what a re-grade reads,
and run dir tells it which run to look in.

44
00:02:34,183 --> 00:02:36,783
There are two limits, and here's the
first.

45
00:02:37,283 --> 00:02:42,278
Runs made before the eval file had a diff
grader kept no copies, so they give a diff

46
00:02:42,278 --> 00:02:43,883
grader nothing to re-grade.

47
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:47,925
The second limit is about script graders.

48
00:02:48,425 --> 00:02:52,900
Diff graders work from the saved diff, so
their results in a re-grade hold.

49
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:57,783
Script graders need the workspace, and a
saved run keeps the diff, not the

50
00:02:57,783 --> 00:02:58,375
workspace.

51
00:02:58,875 --> 00:03:03,998
So in a re-grade, a script grader fails on
every trial, including trials that really

52
00:03:03,998 --> 00:03:04,425
passed.

53
00:03:04,925 --> 00:03:08,275
Ours even says why: there's no changelog
to check.

54
00:03:08,775 --> 00:03:13,100
In a re-grade we ignore script graders,
and test those with the oracle.

55
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:18,930
The saved trials are about to catch a real
mistake, one we made while writing this

56
00:03:18,930 --> 00:03:19,325
course.

57
00:03:19,825 --> 00:03:23,525
When we first wrote no made-up number,
this was its pattern.

58
00:03:24,025 --> 00:03:28,375
The eval file's pattern is the same, plus
four characters at the front.

59
00:03:28,875 --> 00:03:33,555
And it reads correctly: a line that starts
with a plus sign, and contains a number in

60
00:03:33,555 --> 00:03:34,050
brackets.

61
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:36,150
It's wrong, though.

62
00:03:37,367 --> 00:03:40,317
Here's why, on the patch from a saved
trial.

63
00:03:40,817 --> 00:03:45,284
Those four characters are a switch that
makes the caret mean the start of every

64
00:03:45,284 --> 00:03:45,567
line.

65
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:50,117
Without the switch, the caret means only
the very start of the whole patch.

66
00:03:50,617 --> 00:03:55,310
But a patch never starts with a plus sign;
it starts with a header line like this

67
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:55,542
one.

68
00:03:56,042 --> 00:04:00,667
So the pattern can never be found, however
many made-up numbers sit further down.

69
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:05,892
Any grader that passes when its pattern
isn't found will then pass everything.

70
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,650
That's how it works; now let's catch it.

71
00:04:10,150 --> 00:04:14,628
First, we'll re-grade a saved run where we
know failures happened, with the graders

72
00:04:14,628 --> 00:04:15,275
as they are.

73
00:04:15,775 --> 00:04:20,211
Then the flawed pattern goes in, and we
ask the oracle and the saved trials about

74
00:04:20,211 --> 00:04:21,525
it, one after the other.

75
00:04:22,025 --> 00:04:24,475
Last, the right pattern goes back.

76
00:04:24,975 --> 00:04:29,300
What would you expect the oracle to say
about a grader that can never fail?

77
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,675
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

78
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:40,275
For this, the run we want is the first one
we graded with diff graders, made before

79
00:04:40,275 --> 00:04:44,650
the skill had any rule about numbers, and
before the eval file had a script grader.

80
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:49,985
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run.

81
00:04:50,485 --> 00:04:53,385
We change into it, so the paths stay
short.

82
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:00,749
Now the re-grade: the eval file is three
folders up, the run dir is this folder,

83
00:05:00,749 --> 00:05:02,805
and the saved results are the input.

84
00:05:03,305 --> 00:05:07,304
Vally prints one block for each saved
trial: the stimulus name, a tick or a

85
00:05:07,304 --> 00:05:10,930
cross, and each failing grader with its
evidence, but not the model.

86
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:16,239
Eighteen blocks come back, for three
stimuli run three times on two models,

87
00:05:16,239 --> 00:05:18,258
which is more than fits on a screen.

88
00:05:22,467 --> 00:05:27,304
Here are the six blocks for the typo fix
stimulus: three trials on each of the two

89
00:05:27,304 --> 00:05:27,717
models.

90
00:05:28,217 --> 00:05:32,567
Three passed and three failed, the same
count as when the run was first graded.

91
00:05:33,067 --> 00:05:37,674
Two of the failures name no made-up
number: those are the trials where a model

92
00:05:37,674 --> 00:05:38,442
invented one.

93
00:05:38,942 --> 00:05:43,474
The third names adds an entry: in that
trial the model asked for the number, and

94
00:05:43,474 --> 00:05:44,267
wrote nothing.

95
00:05:47,467 --> 00:05:49,992
Next, the flawed pattern goes in.

96
00:05:50,492 --> 00:05:54,282
In the eval file, under the no made-up
number grader, we delete the four

97
00:05:54,282 --> 00:05:56,967
characters from the front of the pattern,
and save.

98
00:05:57,467 --> 00:05:59,992
Nothing else in the file changes.

99
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:05,425
Back in the practice folder, we ask the
oracle about the changed grader.

100
00:06:06,787 --> 00:06:11,635
First with the golden patch applied, for
the typo fix stimulus alone, printing each

101
00:06:11,635 --> 00:06:12,687
grader's evidence.

102
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:22,383
It passed: with a correct answer in place,
the pattern isn't found.

103
00:06:25,883 --> 00:06:29,108
Then without the golden patch, on the
untouched file.

104
00:06:36,067 --> 00:06:40,828
Adds an entry failed as expected, no
made-up number is marked not applicable,

105
00:06:40,828 --> 00:06:42,992
and the last line says baseline OK.

106
00:06:47,492 --> 00:06:49,867
The oracle has no complaint.

107
00:06:52,767 --> 00:06:57,631
So let's ask the saved trials: back into
the run's folder, and the same re-grade as

108
00:06:57,631 --> 00:06:58,042
before.

109
00:07:00,827 --> 00:07:03,902
The only thing that has changed is the
pattern.

110
00:07:07,707 --> 00:07:09,757
Eighteen blocks again.

111
00:07:12,967 --> 00:07:17,746
Side by side, for the six typo fix trials:
with the right pattern, two failed no

112
00:07:17,746 --> 00:07:18,642
made-up number.

113
00:07:19,142 --> 00:07:23,601
With the flawed pattern, none did, not
even the trials where we know a number was

114
00:07:23,601 --> 00:07:24,042
made up.

115
00:07:24,542 --> 00:07:28,836
Five of the six now pass, and the sixth
still fails adds an entry, because it

116
00:07:28,836 --> 00:07:29,617
wrote nothing.

117
00:07:30,117 --> 00:07:34,892
So the oracle accepted this grader, and
the saved trials show that it cannot fail.

118
00:07:38,100 --> 00:07:42,457
Finally, the four characters go back into
the eval file, and we run the re-grade

119
00:07:42,457 --> 00:07:44,200
once more from the run's folder.

120
00:07:50,753 --> 00:07:55,753
These blocks match the first re-grade: no
made-up number fails the trials it should.

121
00:07:59,967 --> 00:08:02,142
So what should we make of that?

122
00:08:02,642 --> 00:08:06,758
Four characters were the difference
between a grader that works and one that

123
00:08:06,758 --> 00:08:07,842
approves everything.

124
00:08:08,342 --> 00:08:11,267
Our oracle runs couldn't tell the two
apart.

125
00:08:11,767 --> 00:08:15,367
Saved trials could, because some of them
were known to be bad.

126
00:08:16,567 --> 00:08:21,292
The same re-grade settled our other
must-not grader, with no extra work.

127
00:08:21,792 --> 00:08:26,530
Adds nothing failed all six refactor
trials, as it should, because both models

128
00:08:26,530 --> 00:08:27,867
recorded the refactor.

129
00:08:28,367 --> 00:08:32,342
So both must-not graders now have saved
trials that they fail.

130
00:08:33,567 --> 00:08:37,667
This is worth making a habit, whenever we
write or change a grader.

131
00:08:38,167 --> 00:08:40,542
First the oracle, which is free.

132
00:08:41,042 --> 00:08:45,845
Then a re-grade of a saved run where the
failure is known to occur, which is also

133
00:08:45,845 --> 00:08:46,142
free.

134
00:08:46,642 --> 00:08:49,367
Only then do we spend agent runs.

135
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:55,039
Before you go on: which kind of grader
can't the oracle test, and what tests it

136
00:08:55,039 --> 00:08:55,492
instead?

137
00:08:58,492 --> 00:09:02,688
Graders that check for absence: an
untouched workspace passes them, so the

138
00:09:02,688 --> 00:09:03,992
oracle can't test them.

139
00:09:04,492 --> 00:09:08,895
What tests it is a re-grade of a saved
trial known to contain the fault: the

140
00:09:08,895 --> 00:09:10,517
grader must fail that trial.

141
00:09:11,733 --> 00:09:16,316
You can now re-grade a saved run without
starting an agent, and show that a grader

142
00:09:16,316 --> 00:09:18,383
the oracle accepted could never fail.

143
00:09:18,883 --> 00:09:23,550
Our graders have been tested against
correct answers, untouched files, and real

144
00:09:23,550 --> 00:09:24,258
bad answers.

145
00:09:24,758 --> 00:09:28,133
But in every trial so far, the skill was
loaded.

146
00:09:28,633 --> 00:09:33,108
But will it load for other wordings, and
stay out of requests that aren't its job?

147
00:09:33,608 --> 00:09:35,458
That's the next lesson.

148
00:09:35,958 --> 00:09:39,533
The exercises on the lesson page are worth
doing first.
